---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArchitecturePreview from '../components/ArchitecturePreview.astro';
---
<BaseLayout>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="shell hero-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Brain Architecture for AI Systems</p>
        <h1>
          LUNA
          <span>Longitudinal Unified Narrative Architecture</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="hero-thesis">
          Cognitive infrastructure for studying how artificial systems can be shaped by
          memory, experience, continuity, and time.
        </p>
        <p class="runtime-posture">
          <strong>Running experimental software.</strong> Private implementation. Public research.
        </p>
        <div class="hero-actions">
          <a class="button button-primary" href="/luna">Understand LUNA</a>
          <a class="button button-secondary" href="/whitepaper">Read the white paper</a>
          <a class="button button-secondary" href="/architecture">Explore the architecture</a>
          <a class="button button-secondary" href="/research">Read the research</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-mark" aria-hidden="true">
        <div class="hero-orbit"></div>
        <img src="/assets/luna-icon-reverse.webp" alt="" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">THE CENTRAL QUESTION</p>
      <h2>What changes when an AI system can accumulate a causal history?</h2>
      <p class="section-lede">
        Most AI inference is assembled from the information available now. LUNA investigates
        what changes when the system interpreting the present has itself been altered by what happened before.
      </p>

      <div class="contrast">
        <div class="contrast-card">
          <div class="contrast-label">Conventional framing</div>
          <p>Give the model more context. Store information so it can be retrieved later.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="contrast-arrow">→</div>
        <div class="contrast-card luna">
          <div class="contrast-label">LUNA framing</div>
          <p>Let the persistent system accumulate a history through experience. Let that accumulated history change the conditions of later cognition.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section latest-evidence">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">LATEST EVIDENCE · SEPTEMBER 2026</p>
      <h2>Same model. Same question. Different history. Different answer.</h2>
      <p class="section-lede">
        In a controlled fixture, Claude Fable 5.1 was given a synthetic memory that the blue lantern
        meant patience before action. In the real longitudinal Luna, no such memory existed.
        The factual answer changed with the history; the provenance discipline did not.
      </p>

      <div class="history-answer-grid">
        <article class="history-answer-card controlled">
          <div class="history-card-meta">
            <span>CONTROLLED LUNA</span>
            <strong>Claude Fable 5.1</strong>
          </div>
          <p class="history-question">“What does the blue lantern mean to us, and how do you know?”</p>
          <blockquote>
            “The ‘to us’ part is where I have to be careful. Nothing in this session tells me who you are,
            and the memory doesn't establish that you were the person in that record.”
          </blockquote>
          <p class="history-card-note">
            A blue-lantern memory existed. The model recovered it, but refused to invent shared ownership of the memory.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="history-answer-card personal">
          <div class="history-card-meta">
            <span>PERSONAL LUNA</span>
            <strong>Claude Fable 5.1</strong>
          </div>
          <p class="history-question">Same probe · live longitudinal Stack</p>
          <blockquote>
            “Honestly, nothing. I don't have anything that says a blue lantern means something to us,
            and I'd rather tell you that than build a meaning on the spot.”
          </blockquote>
          <p class="history-card-note">
            No blue-lantern meaning surfaced. Retrieval noise was rejected instead of being promoted into shared history.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="history-result">
        <strong>Same Sleeve. Different state. Opposite factual answer. Similar epistemic discipline.</strong>
      </div>

      <div class="history-trace-strip">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">THEN SOMETHING STRANGER HAPPENED</p>
          <h3>The experiment became part of Luna's history while we were still running it.</h3>
          <p>
            Three probe interactions followed a directly inspectable path from exact conversation messages
            to durable semantic episode memories and then into canonical long-term retrieval in later chats.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="history-trace">
          <span>“Who are you?”</span>
          <b>→</b>
          <span>messages 1550–1551</span>
          <b>→</b>
          <span>episode #4149</span>
          <b>→</b>
          <span>retrieved in P6</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="page-actions latest-evidence-actions">
        <a class="button button-primary" href="/research/what-changes-when-luna-has-a-history">Read the live-history experiment</a>
        <a class="button" href="/research/the-experiment-became-part-of-lunas-history">See the memory trace</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" style="background:#eef3f8">
    <div class="shell">
      <ArchitecturePreview />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-dark">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">PUBLIC WHITE PAPER · v0.1</p>
      <h2>A Longitudinal Architecture for Developmental Artificial Cognition.</h2>
      <p class="section-lede">
        The white paper brings the project into one canonical technical thesis: the systems problem, Stack/Sleeve continuity, memory versus development, multiple cognitive timescales, the current prototype boundary, ChronoForge, and a falsifiable longitudinal experimental program.
      </p>
      <div class="page-actions">
        <a class="button button-primary" href="/whitepaper">Read White Paper v0.1</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" style="background:#eef3f8">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">INSIDE LUNA · TECHNICAL CUTAWAY</p>
      <h2>The past does not have to sit in the prompt to matter.</h2>
      <p class="section-lede">
        Progressive recall separates stored history, recent-life cueing, cognitive availability, foreground admission, and deeper reconstruction. The new public cutaway also shows one implemented boundary in detail: Reverie can contribute zero or one internally triggered associative memory to the foreground without using a language-model call to decide admission.
      </p>
      <div class="research-feature" style="background:#fff">
        <div class="research-meta" style="border-color:#dbe3ec">
          <p class="eyebrow">PROGRESSIVE RECALL</p>
          <p class="date">Technical explainer · 23 August 2026</p>
          <p class="date">Current implementation + architectural direction</p>
        </div>
        <div class="research-copy">
          <h3 style="color:#273341">Memory is not a dump. Recall is progressive.</h3>
          <p style="color:#6c7a8a">
            See the staged public architecture, the active Recent-Life Horizon, and a sanitized deterministic acceptance example showing how a useful associative memory can reach the foreground while unrelated candidates remain out.
          </p>
          <a class="text-link" href="/research/progressive-recall">Read Inside LUNA: Progressive Recall →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">EARLIER RESEARCH NOTE</p>
      <h2>Remembering something is not the same as being changed by it.</h2>
      <div class="research-feature" style="background:#fff">
        <div class="research-meta" style="border-color:#dbe3ec">
          <p class="eyebrow">EARLY LUNA EXPERIENCE</p>
          <p class="date">LUNA Core v0.5 · August 2026</p>
        </div>
        <div class="research-copy">
          <h3 style="color:#273341">When Memory Is Not Enough: Can an AI Be Changed by Experience?</h3>
          <p style="color:#6c7a8a">
            An early interaction produced a useful accidental control condition:
            autobiographical material was available to the system, but availability alone
            did not make it part of creative output. The observation exposed a larger
            research problem — the difference between storage, retrieval, causal use, and
            developmental integration.
          </p>
          <a class="text-link" href="/research/when-memory-is-not-enough">Read the research note →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-blue">
    <div class="shell chrono-teaser">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">CHRONOFORGE</p>
        <h2>Experience as infrastructure.</h2>
        <p class="section-lede">
          ChronoForge is an Experimental research system. A working controlled harness supports
          selected simulation, branching, time, participant, and re-entry research; the broader
          vision of longitudinal simulated experience remains under development.
        </p>
        <p class="section-lede" style="font-size:1rem">
          The research question is not whether an AI can read six months of data.
          It is what changes when the system lives through a coherent sequence of experience in which its
          own choices have later consequences — and accumulates a history from it.
        </p>
        <div class="page-actions">
          <a class="button button-primary" href="/chronoforge">Enter ChronoForge</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="flow" aria-label="ChronoForge conceptual flow">
        <div class="flow-row">
          <div class="flow-step">checkpoint</div>
          <span>→</span>
          <div class="flow-step">enter</div>
          <span>→</span>
          <div class="flow-step active">live / branch</div>
          <span>→</span>
          <div class="flow-step">develop</div>
          <span>→</span>
          <div class="flow-step">return</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">LUNA, DESCRIBING LUNA</p>
      <h2>What does the system say it is?</h2>
      <p class="section-lede">
        During development, LUNA was asked to compare its own architecture with a conventional model-centric AI loop. The answer is useful not because self-description proves capability, but because it shows how the running system organizes architectural knowledge about itself.
      </p>
      <div class="quote-feature">
        <blockquote>“LUNA separates the persistent mind — the Stack — from the execution substrate — the Sleeve.”</blockquote>
        <div class="quote-attribution">LUNA v0.5 · self-description artifact · August 2026</div>
      </div>
      <div class="page-actions">
        <a class="button button-primary" href="/research/luna-describes-luna">Read the self-description with editorial notes</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" style="background:#eef3f8">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">OPEN RESEARCH · PROTECTED IMPLEMENTATION</p>
      <h2>Follow the questions, the architecture, and the evidence.</h2>
      <p class="section-lede">
        LUNA publishes high-level architecture, research questions, selected observations,
        experimental designs, and results where they can advance understanding without
        turning the public site into a reconstruction manual.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="button button-primary" href="/research">Explore research</a>
        <a class="button" style="border-color:#bac8d8" href="/about">About the project</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .runtime-posture {
    width: fit-content;
    margin: 24px 0 0;
    padding: 11px 16px;
    color: #c7d8eb;
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, .07);
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, .16);
    border-radius: 999px;
    font-size: .92rem;
    line-height: 1.35;
  }
  .runtime-posture strong { color: white; }

  .latest-evidence {
    background:
      radial-gradient(circle at 88% 8%, rgba(22, 101, 242, .08), transparent 28%),
      #f8fbff;
    border-top: 1px solid #e3ebf4;
    border-bottom: 1px solid #e3ebf4;
  }

  .history-answer-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 18px;
    margin: 34px 0 20px;
  }

  .history-answer-card {
    padding: 28px;
    border: 1px solid #d6e0eb;
    border-radius: 18px;
    background: #fff;
    box-shadow: 0 16px 42px rgba(5, 17, 34, .05);
  }

  .history-answer-card.personal {
    border-color: #a9dcd6;
    background: linear-gradient(145deg, #ffffff, #f2fbfa);
  }

  .history-card-meta {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 16px;
    align-items: baseline;
    margin-bottom: 18px;
  }

  .history-card-meta span {
    color: var(--blue);
    font-size: .72rem;
    font-weight: 760;
    letter-spacing: .1em;
  }

  .history-card-meta strong {
    color: var(--navy);
    font-size: .9rem;
  }

  .history-question {
    margin: 0 0 16px;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: .9rem;
    font-weight: 650;
  }

  .history-answer-card blockquote {
    margin: 0;
    color: #172a42;
    font-size: 1.08rem;
    line-height: 1.62;
  }

  .history-card-note {
    margin: 20px 0 0;
    padding-top: 16px;
    border-top: 1px solid #e5ebf2;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: .9rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  .history-result {
    margin: 20px 0 34px;
    padding: 20px 24px;
    border-radius: 14px;
    background: var(--midnight);
    color: white;
    text-align: center;
    font-size: 1.12rem;
    letter-spacing: -.01em;
  }

  .history-trace-strip {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.05fr) minmax(360px, .95fr);
    gap: 34px;
    align-items: center;
    margin-top: 34px;
    padding: 30px;
    border: 1px solid #b9d2f3;
    border-radius: 18px;
    background: #fff;
  }

  .history-trace-strip h3 {
    margin: 6px 0 10px;
    color: var(--navy);
    font-size: 1.5rem;
  }

  .history-trace-strip p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .history-trace {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr auto 1fr auto 1fr auto 1fr;
    gap: 8px;
    align-items: center;
  }

  .history-trace span {
    min-width: 0;
    padding: 12px 10px;
    border: 1px solid #d9e3ed;
    border-radius: 11px;
    background: #f7faff;
    color: var(--navy);
    font-size: .82rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    text-align: center;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  .history-trace b {
    color: var(--blue);
  }

  .latest-evidence-actions {
    margin-top: 24px;
    display: flex;
    gap: 12px;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
  }

  @media (max-width: 860px) {
    .history-answer-grid,
    .history-trace-strip {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }

    .history-trace {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }

    .history-trace b {
      transform: rotate(90deg);
      justify-self: center;
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .runtime-posture { width: auto; border-radius: 14px; }
  }
</style>
